OPEN DESIGN PROBE (RED, off-board): element-obligation move A->B inside a for-loop body — blocked by KORU032 (consume of outer-owned handle inside @scope). See header.

================================================================================
DESIGN RESOLVED (2026-06-21, Lars + Claude). REAL gap, and a STATIC checker
extension — NOT a semantic change, NOT a "dynamic" or runtime obligation.
Obligations are compile-time phantoms, ERASED at emit (see 400_106). Nothing here
is counted at runtime. If an implementation reaches for a runtime counter /
refcount / drain-assert, it has misread the probe — keep it static.

THE SHAPE (why it's actually sound):
  `h0 <owned!>` is owned in the OUTER scope. Inside `for(...)` (an @scope
  boundary), `take(h: h0)` CONSUMES h0 `<!owned>` AND re-issues `<owned!>` on the
  popped value `v`; `put(h: v)` consumes it — all in the SAME iteration. Net across
  the scope boundary the outstanding-obligation count is CONSERVED: the outer
  obligation is consumed, but an equal obligation is issued-and-discharged inside
  the same scope. Linearity holds. This is the "issue it AND consume it in the same
  scope" case — symmetric, conserved.

WHY IT'S RED:
  error[KORU032]: "Cannot discharge outer-scope resource 'h0' inside @scope
  boundary" (auto_discharge:44). The checker rejects the outer-scope consume the
  instant it sees it, WITHOUT checking whether a compensating reissue+discharge
  occurs within the same scope. It stops at the first half of a conserved move.

IMPLEMENTATION TARGET:
  src/auto_discharge_inserter.zig (KORU032 site, ~line 44) and
  src/phantom_semantic_checker.zig (cleanup_obligations tracking). Teach it
  conserved-move recognition: consuming an outer-scope obligation inside an @scope
  is sound when a matching obligation is re-issued and discharged within that same
  scope (net-conserved across the boundary). Static recognition only — no runtime.

CONTRAST (useful for the implementer):
  330_083 is GREEN and looks "harder" (a #loop back-edge) but sidesteps KORU032
  entirely: it issues FRESH handles from opaque host bins each lap, so it never
  consumes an OUTER-scope phantom inside the scope. THIS test is red precisely
  because it consumes the outer obligation in-scope. The fix here is recognizing
  that the outer consume is compensated.

  Sibling probe with the same root: 330_072 (loop BACK-EDGE form, KORU030).
================================================================================
